WEBVTT

00:00:01.220 --> 00:00:06.550
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago and Public Radio International, it's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥WBEZ和国际公共广播电台，欢迎收听《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:47.880
<v Marlene>My name is Marlene. I'm a senior at Lakeview High School, and I've had a crush on this one guy for four years. At first, it was like, OK, he's cute. Yeah, let me get to know him. And then, after a while, it's, OK, I really know you, and you really know me. So do you like me? Like, giving out hints but not really saying anything. And then it goes into-- when you walk down the hall, trying to touch hands to see if he'll actually grab your hand and stuff like that.
<v Marlene>我叫玛琳。我是湖景高中的大四学生，四年来我一直暗恋着一个男生。起初，我觉得，好吧，他挺可爱的。嗯，让我去了解他一下。后来，过了段时间，我又想，好了，我真的了解你了，你也真的了解我了。那你喜欢我吗？就像那种不断发出暗示，却什么都不明说。再后来就变成了——当你走在走廊里，试着去触碰他的手，看看他会不会真的抓住你的手，诸如此类的事情。

00:00:47.880 --> 00:01:09.140
<v Marlene>There's times where it's like that, and you know he doesn't like you. But then, then, there's times like this one time, I sneezed. And he looked at me, he goes, "You have a really cute sneeze." It's like, Oh my god. What are you doing to me? Because it's just totally mixed signals.
<v Marlene>有时候会是那样，你知道他不一定喜欢你。但也有的时候，像有一次，我打了个喷嚏。他看着我说：“你的喷嚏打得真可爱。”我就想，天哪。你到底对我做了什么？因为这完全是混乱的信号。

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:16.230
<v Ira Glass>With a crush, everything is more intense. Everything your crushee says and does is freighted with such meaning.
<v Ira Glass>有了暗恋对象，一切都变得更加强烈了。你暗恋对象所说和所做的一切，都承载着极其深远的意义。

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:40.680
<v Marlene>I was all day on that sneeze comment. I must've told every single one of my friends. And I was like, man, it's this stupid little "you have a cute sneeze" thing. And I was like, man, OK, get over it. Get over it. No big deal. But it was the highlight of my day, man.
<v Marlene>那一整天我都在想那个关于喷嚏的评论。我肯定告诉了我所有的朋友。当时我就想，天哪，这只是个关于“你打喷嚏很可爱”的愚蠢小事。我对自己说，行了，忘掉它吧。忘掉它。没什么大不了的。但那确实是我那天最开心的时刻。

00:01:40.680 --> 00:02:01.100
<v Ira Glass>Having a crush, anybody knows, means being utterly, miserably, dejected and completely filled with hope at the same time. But after four years of an on-again, off-again crush on this guy, just last week, Marlene started seeing somebody else. And she says that compared with dating, a crush is better better in certain ways.
<v Ira Glass>任何人都会明白，暗恋意味着同时处于一种既彻底绝望、又充满希望的矛盾状态。但在断断续续暗恋这个男生四年后，就在上周，玛琳开始和别人约会了。她说，与约会相比，暗恋在某些方面其实更好。

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:32.950
<v Marlene>The whole you don't know who he is, and you don't know if he likes you, and that trying to bump into him every day and stuff. But when you start dating somebody, it's just a normal thing. It's not something spontaneous. If you have a crush, you do anything just to be in his way, and it's just all this spontaneous, exciting thing. You have a boyfriend, it's just like, OK, yeah, OK. It's 3 o'clock. I see you now.
<v Marlene>那种你不知道他是谁，不知道他是否喜欢你，每天试图和他偶遇之类的感觉。但当你开始和某人正式约会时，这一切就变得很平常了。不再那么自发且充满惊喜。如果你在暗恋，你会做任何事来制造偶遇，那一切都是那么自发且令人兴奋。如果你有了男朋友，那就只是，好吧，嗯，好的。现在3点了，我见到你了。

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:45.820
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our program, for Valentine's Day, stories about love in its earliest stage. Crushes. What's terrible about them, what's great about them, and how they can overshadow real love for some people.
<v Ira Glass>好了，在今天的情人节节目中，我们要讲关于爱情最初阶段的故事。暗恋。它们的可怕之处，它们的伟大之处，以及它们如何让某些人的真实爱情黯然失色。

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:52.310
<v Ira Glass>Act One of our program, Kiss. Tobias Wolff has the story of a crush that lasted a lifetime.
<v Ira Glass>节目的第一幕，“吻”。托拜厄斯·沃尔夫带来了关于一段持续了一生的暗恋故事。

00:02:52.310 --> 00:03:05.430
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, How a Bill Becomes-- A President, in which a press secretary for a major presidential campaign explains the dynamics of crushes that are not about love but about politics.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，“法案如何成为——总统”，一位主要总统竞选活动的媒体秘书解释了那些不关乎爱情，而关乎政治的暗恋动力。

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:29.650
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, On Seeing the 100% Perfect Girl One April Morning, in which one lonely crushee finally figures out what it was that he wanted to say-- what we all want to say as the person of our dreams passes us on the street. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，“四月的一个清晨见到百分之百的完美女孩”，一个孤独的暗恋者终于弄清楚了他想表达什么——那是当梦中情人与我们擦肩而过时，我们所有人都想说的话。请继续收听。

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:41.770
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Kiss. So what if you held onto a high school crush? Under what conditions would it never go away? Tobias Wolff has this short story.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，“吻”。如果你一直守着高中的暗恋对象会怎样？在什么情况下它会永远挥之不去？托拜厄斯·沃尔夫带来了这个短篇故事。

00:03:41.770 --> 00:04:11.880
<v Tobias Wolff>When Joe Reed was a boy of 15, his craziness over a girl became such a burden to his family, and such a curiosity to the small town where they lived, that his mother threatened to pack him off to his married sister in San Diego. But before this could happen, Joe's father died, and his mother collected a large sum from Northwestern Mutual and sold the family pharmacy and moved both Joe and herself to California.
<v Tobias Wolff>当乔·里德15岁时，他对一个女孩的痴迷成为了家庭的负担，也成了他们居住的小镇上的奇谈，以至于他的母亲威胁要把他送到圣地亚哥去投奔他已婚的姐姐。但在这件事发生之前，乔的父亲去世了，母亲从西北互助人寿保险公司拿到了一大笔赔偿金，卖掉了家里的药店，带着乔搬到了加利福尼亚。

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:39.660
<v Tobias Wolff>30 years passed. In that time, he heard nothing from the girl, Mary Claude Moore, but now and then, word of her reached him through people back in Dunston. She dropped out of high school in her senior year, had a baby, got married, got divorced, remarried a few years later. That second marriage was the last thing Joe learned about Mary Claude until he got the news of her death.
<v Tobias Wolff>30年过去了。那段时间，他没有收到任何关于那个女孩玛丽·克劳德·摩尔的消息，但偶尔会通过邓斯顿的老相识听到一些关于她的传闻。她大四那年退学了，生了个孩子，结了婚，离了婚，几年后又再婚了。那次再婚是乔在得知玛丽·克劳德死讯前，最后一次听到的关于她的消息。

00:04:39.660 --> 00:05:01.200
<v Tobias Wolff>He dropped by his mother's house one Sunday afternoon. They had coffee together. And that was when she told him about Mary Claude and gave him the letter. He didn't want to be thinking about what his reaction looked like, or ought to look like, so he excused himself and took the letter outside to the backyard.
<v Tobias Wolff>一个周日的下午，他顺道去了母亲家。他们一起喝了咖啡。就在那时，母亲告诉了他玛丽·克劳德的事，并把那封信给了他。他不想去想自己的反应看起来像什么，或者应该看起来像什么，于是他找了个借口，带着信去了后院。

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:18.850
<v Tobias Wolff>According to the newspaper clipping his mother's friend had enclosed, Mary Claude appeared to have fallen asleep at the wheel and drifted into the oncoming lane of traffic. She'd been killed outright, and so had the driver of the car she hit, a dentist from Bellingham on his way home from a weekend of fishing.
<v Tobias Wolff>根据母亲朋友附上的剪报，玛丽·克劳德似乎是在开车时睡着了，漂移到了对面车道。她当场死亡，被她撞上的那辆车的司机也死了，那是一名从贝灵汉钓鱼归来的牙医。

00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:43.680
<v Tobias Wolff>That was the newspaper account. The unofficial version, which his mother's friend disparaged but passed along anyway, was that Mary Claude had been having a fling with a real estate agent named Chip Ryan. Chip Ryan drove the same unusual car as the dentist, a red Mercedes station wagon. And Mary Claude had an equally distinctive old Mustang convertible, powder blue.
<v Tobias Wolff>那是报纸上的报道。而非官方版本，尽管被母亲的朋友所不屑但还是传了出来，是说玛丽·克劳德与一位名叫奇普·瑞安的房产中介有一段情。奇普·瑞安开着和那位牙医一样的罕见车型，一辆红色梅赛德斯旅行车。而玛丽·克劳德则有一辆同样显眼的旧款粉蓝色野马敞篷车。

00:05:43.680 --> 00:06:12.560
<v Tobias Wolff>Both of them lived outside town and frequently passed each other coming and going. The story was, whenever they met on an empty stretch of road, they played a game where they switched lanes at the last moment, a sort of lovers game. Mary Claude had mistaken the dentist's car for Chip's, and that was that.
<v Tobias Wolff>他们都住在镇外，经常在来往时路过对方。传闻说，每当他们在空旷的路上相遇，他们就会玩一种游戏，在最后一刻变换车道，这是一种恋人间的游戏。玛丽·克劳德把牙医的车误认为是奇普的车，事情就是这样。

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:50.400
<v Tobias Wolff>Joe could hardly make sense of the story. His mother's friend doubted it was true, but conceded that it certainly was a puzzle, how Mary Claude could've fallen asleep just 100 feet past a series of tight curves. Still, she wrote, there were probably other ways of explaining it without insulting her memory and giving needless pain to her family. She was survived by her husband, three children, and two grandchildren. For some reason, the paper hadn't run a picture of her with the piece. Joe was glad of the omission.
<v Tobias Wolff>乔几乎无法理解这个故事。母亲的朋友怀疑这是真的，但承认这确实是个谜，玛丽·克劳德怎么会在刚过了一连串急转弯后100英尺的地方就睡着了呢。不过她写道，可能还有其他的解释方式，而不必侮辱她的名声并给她的家人带来不必要的痛苦。她身后留下了丈夫、三个孩子和两个孙辈。出于某种原因，报纸上并没有刊登她的照片。乔对这一疏漏感到庆幸。

00:06:50.400 --> 00:07:16.440
<v Tobias Wolff>Joe had lived another submerged life, parallel to the one known to those around him. In this other life, he hadn't left for California but had stayed on in Dunston with Mary Claude. He fell into this dream during the first months after the move in the immensity of summer, on a sun-struck street full of old geezers, where sprinklers ran at night on lawns visited only by the Mexicans who mowed them.
<v Tobias Wolff>乔过着另一种隐秘的生活，与周围人所知的生活平行。在那另一种生活中，他没有去加利福尼亚，而是留在了邓斯顿，和玛丽·克劳德在一起。搬家后的最初几个月，在漫长的夏天里，他陷入了这个梦境，在一条满是老人的阳光照耀下的街道上，夜里只有修剪草坪的墨西哥人才会光顾的草坪上，喷水装置在嗡嗡作响。

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:43.810
<v Tobias Wolff>When his mother left her darkened bedroom long enough to chase him outside, Joe took the Saturday Evening Post to a pool in a nearby park and watched the girls oil each other and arch their glistening legs over their backs and shriek when loitering thugs threw water on them. He lay on his stomach and stared at the Post and lived his ghost life with Mary Claude.
<v Tobias Wolff>当母亲离开黑暗的卧室赶他出门时，乔就拿着《周六晚报》去附近公园的游泳池，看着女孩们互相涂抹防晒油，将闪亮的双腿拱起搭在背上，当路过的流氓向她们泼水时发出尖叫。他趴在草地上盯着报纸，和玛丽·克劳德过着他的幻影生活。

00:07:43.810 --> 00:08:31.350
<v Tobias Wolff>After Joe started school, his mother took a job as an accountant for an office furniture store. A few months later, she and another woman formed a partnership and bought the owner out. Joe's mother began to dress smartly. She wore her hair straight instead of piled up on her head and let a gray streak show through. One night at dinner, she called "Joe" in such a way he realized she'd been speaking to him without his knowing it. And when he looked at her, she said, "You can't bring him back, son. You have to let him go." Joe was embarrassed at the depth of her misunderstanding, but he played along. He let her think she'd read his mind.
<v Tobias Wolff>乔开始上学后，母亲在一家办公家具店找到了一份会计工作。几个月后，她和另一位女士合伙买下了店主手里的股份。乔的母亲开始打扮得干练起来。她不再把头发盘在头顶，而是把头发拉直，并让灰白的发丝显露出来。一天晚餐时，她喊了声“乔”，乔意识到她在他不知情的情况下已经跟他说了半天话了。当他看着她时，她说：“你没法把他带回来，孩子。你得让他过去。”乔对她这种深刻的误解感到尴尬，但他顺着她的话演了下去。他让她以为她看穿了自己的心事。

00:08:31.350 --> 00:09:01.830
<v Tobias Wolff>The high school was new and bright and vast. In the echoing hallways, the voices of the students mingled in a roar that Joe came to hear as an aspect of the silence in which he passed his days. He sometimes went home without having spoken a word to anyone. It seemed to him that he might go through the whole year that way, and the next year too, until he graduated. But before long, he became friends with his biology lab partner, who took him to parties and introduced him to girls.
<v Tobias Wolff>这所高中又新又明亮且广阔。在回响的走廊里，学生们的谈话声汇成一阵喧嚣，乔开始将其视为他度过时日的那种寂静的一部分。他有时回家时没对任何人说过一句话。他觉得他可能会就这样过完这一年，下一年也一样，直到毕业。但没过多久，他和生物实验室的伙伴成了朋友，对方带他去参加派对，并向他介绍了女孩。

00:09:01.830 --> 00:09:24.090
<v Tobias Wolff>When Joe got his driver's license that spring, he began to date Carla. He aced his courses and played Officer Krupke in West Side Story . In the fall of his senior year, he and Carla left a dance early and went to a motel. It was the first time for both of them and a failure. They tried again a few days later in Carla's bedroom and had better luck.
<v Tobias Wolff>那年春天，乔拿到驾照后开始和卡拉约会。他课程成绩优异，还在《西区故事》里演了克鲁普克警官。大四那年秋天，他和卡拉早早离场，去了一家汽车旅馆。那是他们俩的第一次，结果却是一场失败。几天后他们在卡拉的卧室里再次尝试，运气好了一些。

00:09:24.090 --> 00:09:36.320
<v Tobias Wolff>And by Christmas, Joe was starting to see Courtney on the sly. He didn't really prefer her, but it seemed inevitable that sooner or later, either he or Carla would be unfaithful, and he wanted to be the one.
<v Tobias Wolff>到了圣诞节，乔开始悄悄和考特尼来往。他其实并不更偏爱她，但似乎迟早他和卡拉之间总有一个人会不忠，他想做那个不忠的人。

00:09:36.320 --> 00:10:11.040
<v Tobias Wolff>It turned out to be much more complicated than he'd thought. Joe was soon exposed and denounced by both girls as a heartless cheat, which did not, it turned out, entirely discourage other girls from going out with him. And through all this, he continued his phantom life with Mary Claude. He was with her on a blanket in a moonlit clearing, or in a car parked above the river with Ray Charles on the radio, her fingertips grazing the back of his neck, her mouth open to his, her caramel taste on his lips and tongue and deep in his throat.
<v Tobias Wolff>事情证明比他想象的要复杂得多。乔很快被两个女孩揭发并谴责为无情的骗子，但这并没有完全阻止其他女孩和他约会。尽管如此，他依然继续着他和玛丽·克劳德的幻影生活。他在月光下的空地上和她铺着毯子，或者在停在河边的车里，收音机里放着雷·查尔斯的歌，她的指尖掠过他的后颈，她的嘴唇贴向他，她的焦糖味留在他嘴唇和舌尖，并深入他的喉咙。

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:26.990
<v Tobias Wolff>Only the kiss was a memory. Only the kiss was real. He'd hardly been anywhere with Mary Claude, except when they could sneak off at school and a few times in town. But from the kiss, he made everything else, or everything else made itself.
<v Tobias Wolff>只有那个吻是记忆。只有那个吻是真实的。他和玛丽·克劳德几乎没去过什么地方，除了在学校偷偷溜走的那几次和镇上的几次。但从那个吻开始，他创造了一切，或者说一切自然而然地发生了。

00:10:26.990 --> 00:10:55.010
<v Tobias Wolff>For that was how it happened. Without any effort of imagination or sense of unreality, he watched his life with Mary Claude go on as he had once believed it would. The scenes grew more particular as time passed, each new one framed by those that had gone before, and always with a kiss at the heart of it.
<v Tobias Wolff>事情就是这样发生的。没有丝毫的想象力或虚幻感，他看着他和玛丽·克劳德的生活像他曾经相信的那样继续下去。随着时间的推移，场景变得愈发具体，每一个新场景都由前一个场景串联起来，且核心始终是一个吻。

00:10:55.010 --> 00:11:30.750
<v Tobias Wolff>At Berkeley, Joe went with Maureen. And when Maureen left for a year at the Sorbonne, there was Linda, then Candace. He and Candace shared a house with two other couples until they graduated. And afterward, they rented an apartment of their own through Joe's first year of medical school. Then Candace went to New York to visit her family and never came back. She sent Joe a letter in which she asked his forgiveness for the problems she'd caused through her alcoholism, which she was now in the process of confronting. She said she couldn't return to the life she'd led in Berkeley, as he must understand.
<v Tobias Wolff>在伯克利，乔和莫琳交往。后来莫琳去索邦大学待了一年，接着是琳达，然后是坎迪丝。他和坎迪丝在毕业前一直和另外两对伴侣合租一栋房子。后来，他们在乔上医学院的第一年里租了一套自己的公寓。再后来，坎迪丝去纽约看望家人，就再也没回来。她给乔寄来一封信，请求他原谅她因酗酒而造成的问题，她现在正在面对这个问题。她说她无法回到她在伯克利过的那种生活，他一定能理解。

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:59.640
<v Tobias Wolff>No, Joe did not understand. They'd had their troubles, the two of them. He'd been going all out, and so had Candace, waitressing nights as she worked toward a degree in dance therapy. Of course, there were problems, but nothing that serious. He certainly didn't begrudge her a little relaxation. But when Joe's mother heard about Candace leaving, the first thing she said was that she hoped she'd get some help for her drinking. Joe hadn't mentioned the letter.
<v Tobias Wolff>不，乔不理解。他们之间有过摩擦，他俩都有过。他全力以赴，坎迪丝也一样，一边做服务生，一边攻读舞蹈治疗学位。当然，是有问题，但没那么严重。他当然不会计较她偶尔放松一下。但当乔的母亲听说坎迪丝离开时，她说的第一句话是希望她能为酗酒寻求帮助。乔没提过那封信的事。

00:11:59.640 --> 00:12:18.100
<v Tobias Wolff>By the time he started his residency in Seattle, he'd entered a state of near quarantine that made his shadow life more moony and detailed than ever.
<v Tobias Wolff>当他在西雅图开始实习医生生涯时，他进入了一种近乎隔离的状态，这让他的影子生活比以往任何时候都更加梦幻和详尽。

00:12:18.100 --> 00:12:46.160
<v Tobias Wolff>Dunston was just three hours north of Seattle. Joe sometimes thought of driving up on a free afternoon but never did. By then, he'd heard that Mary Claude was married again. There was no purpose in making the trip except to see her, and he was afraid that she wouldn't want to see him, and also afraid that she would. It was too late for that. She had a daughter and a husband and a house to run. She had work to do.
<v Tobias Wolff>邓斯顿就在西雅图以北三小时路程的地方。乔有时想在空闲的下午开车去看看，但从来没去过。那时，他听说玛丽·克劳德又结婚了。除了去见她之外，此行没有任何目的，而他害怕她不想见他，也害怕她会想见他。已经太迟了。她有女儿、有丈夫，还要打理一个家。她有自己的工作要做。

00:12:46.160 --> 00:13:05.260
<v Tobias Wolff>So did he. Useful, exacting work. It depended on a clarity Joe knew he couldn't rely on, that he had to improvise day by day. He'd lost it before, and could not risk losing it again.
<v Tobias Wolff>他也是。既有用又严苛的工作。它取决于一种乔知道自己无法依赖的清晰感，这种清晰感他必须每天临场发挥。他以前失去过它，不敢再冒失去它的风险。

00:13:05.260 --> 00:13:40.300
<v Tobias Wolff>When Mary Claude was killed, Joe had been married for 17 years. His wife, Liz, was a pediatrician in the same clinic where he practiced as an internist. They had a son in his junior year of high school, a daughter a year younger. The boy was a gifted cellist, unworldly, dreamy. Joe's daughter was more calculating but fiercer in her attachments once she'd made them. Joe began taking her rock climbing when she was still in grade school, and she turned out to be the most fearless and inventive partner he'd ever had.
<v Tobias Wolff>玛丽·克劳德去世时，乔已经结婚17年了。他的妻子利兹和他在同一家诊所担任内科医生。他们有一个读高二的儿子，一个比他小一岁的女儿。男孩是个天才大提琴手，不谙世事，充满幻想。乔的女儿则更精明，一旦投入感情就会变得非常热烈。乔从她读小学起就开始带她去攀岩，她最终成了他见过的最无畏、最有创造力的搭档。

00:13:40.300 --> 00:14:03.600
<v Tobias Wolff>Then came a time when his daughter ceased to confide. Both daughter and son developed private sources of amusement, and Joe began to detect a certain condescension in their handling of him. His children were slipping away into the deep forest. He tried not to hurry them with the panic he felt at the gathering signs of their departure.
<v Tobias Wolff>后来，女儿不再向他倾诉了。儿子和女儿都有了各自的私人娱乐，乔开始察觉到他们对待他时带有一种某种优越感。他的孩子们正溜向森林深处。他尽量不表现出自己在见到他们离去的迹象时感到的恐慌，也不想催促他们。

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:35.310
<v Tobias Wolff>Liz, too, kept changing on him. When they first met, she was girlish and unsure of herself in spite of being three years older than Joe. But since then, she'd grown calm and regal, which both unsettled and excited him. In their lovemaking, he approached her almost wistfully, and sometimes concluded with a bark of triumph, as if he'd brought a notorious virgin to ground. Away from her for more than a day or two, Joe hardly knew who he was.
<v Tobias Wolff>利兹也在不断改变。他们刚认识时，尽管利兹比乔大三岁，但她还是充满少女气且对自己不自信。但从那以后，她变得冷静而庄重，这既让他不安又让他兴奋。在他们的性生活中，他几乎是以一种渴望的姿态接近她，有时还会带着一声胜利的叫喊结束，仿佛他把一个著名的处女降服了一样。只要离开她超过一两天，乔几乎就不知道自己是谁了。

00:14:35.310 --> 00:15:03.880
<v Tobias Wolff>And still, through all these years, he had thoughts of Mary Claude. He thought of Mary Claude sitting across from him at a table in the kitchen, barely awake, drinking coffee. He thought of them standing on a porch and waving as friends drove off. And when they were alone, Mary Claude turned to him and slipped an arm around his waist. And they went slowly inside and up the stairs, stopping to kiss on the landing.
<v Tobias Wolff>尽管如此，这些年来，他仍常会想起玛丽·克劳德。他想到玛丽·克劳德坐在厨房桌子对面，还没完全醒透，喝着咖啡。他想到他们站在门廊上，挥手送别朋友离去。当他们独处时，玛丽·克劳德转过身，将手臂滑向他的腰部。他们慢慢地走进屋里，沿着楼梯往上走，在楼梯口停下来接吻。

00:15:03.880 --> 00:15:28.040
<v Tobias Wolff>Sometimes, Joe thought of what was to follow, but this was the moment he lingered on. The kiss. Joe remembered very well what it was like to kiss Mary Claude. He had done it as much as anyone could for as long as they were going together, which came to just over three months.
<v Tobias Wolff>有时，乔会想接下来会发生什么，但他总是留恋那个时刻。那个吻。乔记得很清楚吻玛丽·克劳德是什么感觉。在他们交往的三个月多一点的时间里，他尽可能多地吻过她。

00:15:28.040 --> 00:15:53.670
<v Tobias Wolff>Mary Claude's father owned a dairy farm several miles out of town. Her mother had moved away when Mary Claude was 11, taking her daughter with her. She married again, but things did not go well between her daughter and this new husband, and she sent Mary Claude back to her father when she was 15. Joe had gone through the first six years of grade school barely noticing her, drab little hick that she was.
<v Tobias Wolff>玛丽·克劳德的父亲在镇外几英里处拥有一家奶牛场。她母亲在玛丽·克劳德11岁时就搬走了，带着女儿一起。她再婚了，但女儿和这位新继父相处得并不好，于是她在玛丽·克劳德15岁时把她送回了父亲那里。乔在小学的前六年里几乎没注意到她，她那时是个土里土气的小村姑。

00:15:53.670 --> 00:16:22.160
<v Tobias Wolff>But she came back a different girl, witchy and vivid. She mouthed off to the teachers and walked around in a pout with her back arched like a bow. She had no friends except for an equally friendless cousin. During volleyball games in her gym class, she baited the other girls by deliberately hitting the ball lot of bounds or into the net. She cut classes and smoked and made out with other girls' boyfriends, or so it was said.
<v Tobias Wolff>但她回来时像换了个人，妖娆而生动。她对老师顶嘴，走起路来嘟着嘴，背拱得像一张弓。除了一个同样没朋友的表亲，她没有朋友。在体育课的排球比赛中，她故意把球打出界或打进网里来戏弄其他女孩。她逃课、抽烟，还和别人的男朋友亲热，据说是这样。

00:16:22.160 --> 00:16:52.190
<v Tobias Wolff>And Joe, curious to test the rumor, found it to be true. Mary Claude went behind the school with him, during a dance to which he'd brought another girl, and kept him out there for over an hour. He knew she was doing it to shame his date, at first anyway, until she warmed to him. But once he started, he couldn't stop kissing her.
<v Tobias Wolff>乔出于好奇想验证一下谣言，发现是真的。在一次舞会上，乔带了另一个女孩，但玛丽·克劳德躲在学校后面把他拉过去，让他待了一个多小时。起初，他知道她这么做是为了让他的舞伴难堪，直到她对他有了兴致。但一旦开始，他就无法停止吻她。

00:16:52.190 --> 00:17:28.550
<v Tobias Wolff>Joe had, by then, kissed several girls and thought he had a pretty fair idea of the possibilities of a kiss. Kissing was good. But he tended to think of it as a beachhead from which to launch more serious operations, and a safe haven when, inevitably, he was forced to retreat. But he didn't remember to try anything else that night, leaning against the gymnasium wall with this girl, who tasted so good and pressed so fully against him, humming in his ear when they stopped to breathe and swaying to the music that rattled the high windows above them.
<v Tobias Wolff>乔在那之前已经吻过几个女孩，自认为对亲吻的各种可能相当了解。接吻的感觉很好。但他往往把接吻看作是发动更严重行动的滩头堡，或者当他不可避免地被迫撤退时的安全港。但那天晚上，靠在健身房的墙上，面对这个味道如此美好、身体紧紧贴着他、在他们停下来喘息时在他耳边哼唱、随着头顶震动高窗的音乐摇摆的女孩，他甚至没想起要尝试别的什么。

00:17:28.550 --> 00:18:19.380
<v Tobias Wolff>There were other couples along the wall, and Joe knew his date would hear about this. But when he started to lean away, Mary Claude laid her fingers along his cheeks and guided his mouth back to hers. And after that, he forgot leaving. He would have stayed there all night with no sense of time passing, but finally, a girl came out and told Mary Claude that her ride was waiting to take her home. She turned to go, then stopped and kissed Joe again. He walked around the school twice before going back inside. The gym was almost empty. His date had left with friends.
<v Tobias Wolff>墙边还有其他情侣，乔知道他的舞伴会听到风声。但当他开始向后倾斜时，玛丽·克劳德把手指搭在他的脸颊上，引导他的嘴回到她嘴上。在那之后，他忘了离开。他本可以在那里待上一整晚，完全没有时间流逝的感觉，但最后，一个女孩走出来告诉玛丽·克劳德，接她回家的车到了。她转身离开，又停下来再次吻了乔。他在学校周围走了两圈才回到室内。健身房里几乎空无一人。他的舞伴已经和朋友们离开了。

00:18:19.380 --> 00:18:55.480
<v Tobias Wolff>When he saw Mary Claude in the hallway on Monday morning, he did not pretend that nothing had happened, nor did she. She'd let him take her books and walk her to class. At the lunch period, they went to the cafeteria and sat across from each other. He understood what would happen, the hush around them, how they'd looked at, even by his friends. Joe knew the rules. He'd been a [BLEEP] and hurt a nice girl. And for Mary Claude, of all people. You could make out with Mary Claude, but you had to laugh about it later and cut her dead.
<v Tobias Wolff>周一早上在走廊见到玛丽·克劳德时，他没装作什么都没发生，她也没有。她让他拿上她的书，陪她去上课。午饭时间，他们去了食堂，面对面坐着。他明白会发生什么，周围的寂静，甚至他朋友们投来的目光。乔知道规则。他是个混蛋，伤害了一个好女孩。而且还是为了玛丽·克劳德。你可以和玛丽·克劳德亲热，但你之后必须对此大加嘲笑，并彻底冷落她。

00:18:55.480 --> 00:19:08.080
<v Tobias Wolff>They ate without talking. Her color was high. Otherwise, she gave nothing away. She helped herself to his carrots, and that was that. They were a pair.
